  "fixture": "FX-SYNC-002",
  "question": "Which process the object manager raises a handle's duplication in - pushed by its source, pulled by its target, or made between two others by a third - at which version and with what its record names of the two ends, and where it raises the records of the handle a duplication made; and, machine-wide, where the records of the synchronization types' kernel handles are raised.",
  "answer": "In 4 runs of 16 rounds, machine-wide and nothing lost, each of the three duplications was recorded once, at its object's address, at version 3: 26 bytes, the 22 the layout the machine registers at version 2 describes - the object, the source's handle value, the handle it made, its target process and the type - and four more after the type, which named the process it was duplicated from in every one. Each was raised in the process that asked for it: the creator's push of its event's handle in the creator, the opener's pull of the semaphore's in the opener, and the coordinator's duplication of the mutex's, between the two, in the coordinator. So a duplication's header names the process that asked for it, and its fourth field, which no layout the machine registers describes, the process it came from. Every duplication of the machine, of every type, was such a record, 2,847 to 3,949 a run. No duplication raised a creation of the handle it made, and each such handle's close was raised in its target, naming the object. Machine-wide, the synchronization types' kernel handles - their value's top bit set - were created in 1 to 3 processes' contexts and closed in 11 to 103, never the system process's, duplicated into the system process's table from 8 to 96, and the stop's rundown named the system process the holder of every one, 1,851 to 1,859, raising its records in 4 other processes' contexts.",
  "method": {
    "session": "a private system logger of the probe's own, its kernel flag Process, with PERF_OB_HANDLE added to its group masks by its handle",
    "workload": "src/InterCat.TestWorkloads sync-objects --rounds 16 --hold-ms 5 --duplicate: FX-SYNC-001's creator and opener, the opener also given a handle of each object by a duplication before the rounds - the creator duplicating its event's handle into the opener, the opener duplicating the semaphore's from the creator into itself, and the coordinator, which started both, duplicating the mutex's from the creator into the opener - each logged by the process that asked for it with both ends; the opener closes each at its end, after its own",
    "reading": "each handle record read from its payload by the layout the machine registers, its header's version and process and its payload's length beside it; a duplication's 32-bit value after its type, which that layout does not describe, read where its payload holds one and named by which of the duplication's processes it is, never by its value; a duplication found at its object's address - the address of the creator's close, naming the object, of the handle it started from - by the target process and handle the truth logged; a kernel handle by its value's top bit"
  },
